# Raipur Urban Heat Island Study
### Hyperlocal thermal mapping of Raipur city using a 
### ground-level sensor network and Landsat 9 NDVI analysis

**Arnav Shekhar · Class 11 PCM · Bhavan's R.K. Sarda Vidya Mandir, Raipur**  
**Mentored by Prof. Samir Bajpai, Department of Civil Engineering, NIT Raipur**

---

## What this project is

Raipur is one of India's fastest-growing cities. Satellite studies 
have established that the city as a whole is warming — but no 
ground-level dataset exists that maps *where within Raipur* the 
heat burden is highest, at street scale, and by how much.

This study deploys a network of 8 DHT22 temperature and humidity 
sensors across ecologically distinct urban zones in Raipur — 
industrial, commercial, residential, and green/water areas — 
logging continuous data at 10-minute intervals over a 3-month 
period (July–September 2026). Sensor readings are correlated with 
Landsat 9 satellite-derived NDVI (Normalised Difference Vegetation 
Index) to quantify the relationship between vegetation cover and 
near-surface air temperature at street level.

The result is the first hyperlocal, ground-truth thermal map of 
Raipur — data that no satellite can produce at this resolution.

---

## Research question

*Which neighbourhoods of Raipur experience the most severe heat 
burden, and does vegetation cover predict near-surface air 
temperature at street level?*

---

## Sensor locations

| Site | Location | Type |
|------|----------|------|
| S1 | Amanaka Industrial Area | Industrial |
| S2 | Pandri Commercial Market | Dense commercial |
| S3 | Shastri Chowk | Urban core |
| S4 | VIP Road Corridor | Arterial / mixed |
| S5 | Shankar Nagar | Mid-density residential |
| S6 | Devendra Nagar | Low-density residential |
| S7 | NIT Raipur Campus | Institutional / semi-green |
| S8 | Telibandha Lake | Water body / urban green |

---

## Methods

- **Hardware:** DHT22 sensors + Arduino Uno + SD card logging
- **Sampling:** Every 10 minutes, continuous, July–September 2026
- **Satellite data:** Landsat 9 OLI-2 Level-2 Surface Reflectance
  (USGS EarthExplorer, <10% cloud cover, monthly)
- **Analysis:** Python (pandas, rasterio, numpy, matplotlib, scipy)
- **Validation:** Cross-referenced against NIT Raipur 
  meteorological station data

